YAML File Creation & Structure

The session began with creating a Kubernetes YAML file from scratch (no cloning), using .yml extension. 12
A consolidated/mono YAML file was written containing all microservices — deployments, services, and service accounts — resulting in ~900 lines (70 lines per microservice × 10 microservices). 34
Key Concepts Covered

Labels & Selectors: Used to integrate/connect microservices with each other. 567
kubectl create vs kubectl apply: create is used the first time; apply is used for subsequent updates. 8
Single-file deployment: One command (kubectl create -f kubernetes-manifest.yml) deploys all microservices at once — enabling single-click deployment. 91011
Mono Repo concept: Introduced as a modern approach (emerged ~3 years ago) for single-repo, single-deployment strategy. 1213
Live Deployment on GKE

A GKE cluster was connected, the repo was cloned, and the manifest was applied. 91415
All pods, deployments, and services were verified via kubectl get pod, kubectl get deploy, and kubectl get all. 161718
Resilience Demo

The payment service pod was deleted to demonstrate downtime; it auto-recovered via the Deployment controller. 192021
Solution discussed: increase replicas for critical services (e.g., front-end: 3–5 replicas) to achieve zero downtime. 222324
Auto-scaling will be covered in the next class. 25
Deployment vs. Service

